<svelte:head>
	<title>Map • How It Works • PARA Docs</title>
</svelte:head>

<section class="docs-hero-card">
	<p class="docs-hero-kicker">How it works</p>
	<h1>Map</h1>
	<p>
		The map turns political data into something spatially legible. States, federal districts, cities,
		and live civic activity are rendered as browsable layers over Mexico. This is not a generic
		location feature — it is a civic intelligence surface.
	</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">Routes</p>
	<h2>Primary routes</h2>
	<div class="docs-meta-grid">
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Map</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value"><code>/map</code>, <code>/map?state=:state&layer=:layer</code>, <code>/map?state=:state&city=:city</code></div>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">District profile</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value"><code>/district-profile/:districtId?tab=overview|activity</code></div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">Layers</p>
	<h2>Four map layers</h2>
	<div class="docs-meta-grid">
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">States</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">All 32 Mexican states as selectable polygons. Selecting a state opens its summary sidebar with dominant party, approval, and active civic metrics.</div>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Districts</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">300 federal electoral districts. Each shows turnout, registered voters, current deputy, and dominant party. Districts can be opened for full profile detail.</div>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Cities</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">Major cities per state with population, dominant party, and governing mayor. City selection zooms to municipal scale.</div>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Civic</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">Density overlay of active cabildeos per state. Color intensity scales with the number of open civic deliberations in each region.</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">Search</p>
	<h2>Find states, districts, and cities</h2>
	<p>
		The map search indexes all 32 states, 300 federal districts, and every major city. It handles
		accent-insensitive text, aliases (CDMX, DF, Edomex), and partial matching. Districts can be
		found by number ("Distrito 7") or by deputy name.
	</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">Zone filters</p>
	<h2>Heatmap lenses</h2>
	<p>
		Users can apply a discourse lens to color the map by political topic. Choose between Matter or
		Policy, then select a specific item. States tint based on how much that topic is discussed
		locally. This turns the map from a geographic view into an ideological distribution view.
	</p>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">State ownership</p>
	<h2>What this route family owns</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>GeoJSON rendering of Mexico at state, district, and city granularity.</li>
		<li>Indexed civic data overlay (cabildeo density, party dominance).</li>
		<li>Search and alias resolution for Mexican political geography.</li>
		<li>Geolocation-based "Near me" routing (Mexico-only).</li>
		<li>District profile deep-links with overview and activity tabs.</li>
	</ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section-card">
	<p class="docs-section-kicker">District profiles</p>
	<h2>What a district profile contains</h2>
	<div class="docs-meta-grid">
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Overview</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">Party breakdown, turnout percentage, registered voter count, current deputy, and open cabildeos or RAQ questions scoped to the district.</div>
		</div>
		<div class="docs-meta-card">
			<span class="docs-meta-label">Activity</span>
			<div class="docs-meta-value">Timeline of civic actions in the district: cabildeo participation, RAQ responses, and community engagement metrics.</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

